diff --git a/docs/user/eebus.md b/docs/user/eebus.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..6b021f3a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/user/eebus.md
@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+# EEBUS
+
+EEBUS is a standard for energy devices in a building to talk to each other.
+With it, the charger can be limited and read by
+
+- the grid operator's control box (in Germany: §14a EnWG, "Steuerbox"), or
+- an energy manager (HEMS) that speaks EEBUS.
+
+The charger offers two EEBUS use cases:
+
+| Use case | Role of the charger | What it does |
+|---|---|---|
+| LPC, Limitation of Power Consumption | Controllable System | The other side sets a limit in watts |
+| MPC, Monitoring of Power Consumption | Monitored Unit | The other side reads power and energy |
+
+EEBUS is only part of firmware builds made for it (`ENABLE_EEBUS`), for
+example `kinetos-eebus-v5`. On the 4 MB boards it takes the place of OCPP,
+because both do not fit into the flash. The build is based on
+[openeebus](https://github.com/NIBEGroup/openeebus) by NIBE.
+
+## Setting it up
+
+1. Open **Settings → EEBUS** and turn on **Enable EEBUS**. The charger needs
+   a network connection. On the first start it creates its own certificate;
+   this takes a few seconds.
+2. The page shows the **SKI** of the charger, 40 hex characters. This is its
+   identity.
+3. Pair the two devices. Both sides have to trust each other:
+   - On the charger: pick the other device under **Found in the network** and
+     press **Pair**, or type its SKI under **Pair by SKI**.
+   - On the other device: enter the SKI of the charger. For a control box the
+     grid operator or metering company does this.
+4. The other device then shows up under **Paired devices** as *Connected*.
+
+EEBUS uses TCP port 4712. The devices find each other with mDNS (UDP 5353),
+so they have to be in the same network.
+
+Up to 8 devices can be paired. Devices that are not paired cannot connect.
+
+## The power limit
+
+The device that sets a limit is called the *energy guard*. The charger marks
+it in the list of paired devices. Without an energy guard the charger is never
+limited by EEBUS.
+
+The limit is a power in watts. The charger turns it into a charge current, the
+same way as [grid operator dimming](grid-dimming.md):
+
+```
+current = limit / (230 V × phases)     (rounded down)
+```
+
+If the limit is too low for the minimum current (usually 6 A, that is 1380 W
+on one phase and 4140 W on three), the charger stops charging until the limit
+is raised or lifted. All other features keep working, but they cannot go above
+the limit. If grid operator dimming is active at the same time, the lower of
+the two limits applies.
+
+### States
+
+| State (`eebus_state`) | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `disabled` | EEBUS is off or no energy guard is paired |
+| `init` | After a start, until the energy guard is heard |
+| `unlimited_controlled` | Energy guard connected, no limit |
+| `limited` | A limit applies, until its duration ends or it is lifted |
+| `failsafe` | The energy guard was not heard for 120 s: the failsafe limit applies |
+| `unlimited_autonomous` | The failsafe duration is over and the energy guard is still gone |
+
+### Failsafe
+
+If the energy guard is not heard for 120 seconds, the charger limits itself to
+the **failsafe limit** for the **failsafe duration**. When the energy guard
+comes back and sends a new limit, the failsafe state ends at once.
+
+The defaults are 4200 W and 2 hours. The energy guard can overwrite both; the
+charger keeps the values it was sent.
+
+## Measurements
+
+The charger reports its total power and its energy counter. With a
+three-phase meter (`ENABLE_SDM_METER`) it also reports power, current and
+voltage per phase. The values are updated every 5 seconds.
+
+## Checking the state
+
+`GET /eebus` returns the state, the SKI and the devices:
+
+```json
+{"enabled":true,"running":true,"ski":"d821f6a40448720a57def41308b191ff94f339a6","port":4712,
+ "state":"limited","limited":true,"limit":4200,"remaining":7150,"heartbeat":true,
+ "failsafe_limit":4200,"failsafe_duration":7200,
+ "peers":[{"ski":"d8d8a68526bb20370327d840fdfd1d78b99953b8","name":"Steuerbox","brand":"","model":"","type":"",
+           "trusted":true,"discovered":true,"connected":true,"waiting":false,"guard":true}]}
+```
+
+`POST /eebus/trust` and `POST /eebus/untrust` pair and unpair; the body is the
+SKI of the other device.
+
+`/status` and the WebSocket also report:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `eebus_state` | State, see the table above |
+| `eebus_limited` | `true` while a limit from EEBUS applies |
+| `eebus_limit` | That limit in W, `0` otherwise |
+
+While the limit applies, `dimming_active` is `true` and `dimming_source` is
+`3`.
+
+## Settings
+
+| Option | Type | Default | Description |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `eebus_enabled` | bool | `false` | Turn EEBUS on |
+| `eebus_failsafe_limit` | W | `4200` | Limit in the failsafe state |
+| `eebus_failsafe_duration` | s | `7200` | How long the failsafe state lasts at least (2 to 24 hours) |
+| `eebus_trusted` | text | empty | SKIs of the paired devices, separated by commas. Use the pairing buttons or `/eebus/trust` to change it |
+| `eebus_guard` | text | empty | SKI of the energy guard. Set by the charger |
+
+The certificate and its key are stored on the charger and are not part of the
+configuration. A factory reset removes them; the charger then gets a new SKI
+and has to be paired again.
+
+## Limits of this implementation
+
+- Tested on a PC against the reference programs of openeebus (the same code
+  as in the firmware). It has not been tested with a control box of a grid
+  operator and it is not certified by the EEBus Initiative.
+- EEBUS takes about 225 KB of flash.
